    text: "Rights and regulations form the framework that protects individuals and governs society. In the UK, fundamental rights include freedom of speech, freedom of religion, the right to a fair trial, the right to privacy, and protection from discrimination. The Human Rights Act 1998 incorporated the European Convention on Human Rights into UK law. Employment rights protect workers: the national minimum wage, statutory sick pay, maternity and paternity leave, protection from unfair dismissal, and the right to join a trade union. Consumer rights ensure product quality and fair treatment: the Consumer Rights Act 2015 covers goods that must be of satisfactory quality, fit for purpose, and as described. The Equality Act 2010 prohibits discrimination based on nine protected characteristics: age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage, pregnancy, race, religion, sex, and sexual orientation. Health and safety regulations protect workers from dangerous conditions — employers must conduct risk assessments, provide safety equipment, and maintain safe premises. Data protection — governed by the UK GDPR and the Data Protection Act 2018 — gives individuals rights over their personal data: the right to access, correct, and delete information. Environmental regulations control pollution, waste disposal, and emissions. Understanding your rights and the regulations that govern daily life empowers you to challenge injustice, make informed decisions, and participate fully in society.",
